llama2.c — Andrej Karpathy
https://github.com/karpathy/llama2.c   (MIT)

    The model was trained with llama2.c: its architecture (model.py) and
    its training loop (train.py), modified to use ternary weights.

    No code from llama2.c is redistributed in this repository. Training is
    reproduced by cloning it and applying two patches:

        git clone https://github.com/karpathy/llama2.c
        python patch_train.py     # inserts the BitLinear substitution
        python patch_sample.py

    src/patch_train.py quotes two single lines from upstream train.py as
    the anchors it searches for. That is the whole of the upstream code
    present here, and a patch cannot name its target otherwise.

    src/generate.c and src/generate_fx.c are independent implementations,
    not ports of run.c: they read this project's own TC64 format and share
    with run.c only the standard names of the operations — rmsnorm,
    softmax, forward. They contain none of its structures.


TinyStories — Ronen Eldan, Yuanzhi Li
https://huggingface.co/datasets/roneneldan/TinyStories   (CDLA-Sharing-1.0)

    The model in src/model_ternary.bin was trained on TinyStories, and
    src/vocab.txt is a 512-token SentencePiece vocabulary built from it.

    Section 3.5 of CDLA-Sharing-1.0 states that the agreement "imposes no
    obligations or restrictions on Your Use or Publication of Results",
    where Results are "the outcomes or outputs that You obtain from Your
    Computational Use of Data". Trained weights are Results. The credit
    here is given because it is owed, not because the licence requires it.


oscar64 — Dr Mortal Wombat
https://github.com/drmortalwombat/oscar64

    The C compiler for the 6502 that this project targets. Not
    redistributed; required to build. Several of its behaviours are
    documented in the README because they shaped the code.


